# ===== CHAPTER 7: Not Signing for the Ditch
# ============================== YEAR 13 ==============================
R(13, "A bad harvest", "dribble2")
act("Silke, the Scholar, turns the I Ching card: a Bad Harvest. Every province collects a bad harvest, and the population holds at x3, 300 million.", map_hl=[4])
say("Silke", "\"Bad Harvest. The seed rotted, the rains came late, and the ox went lame. All provinces collect a bad harvest.\" Every one of us, me included. The counter stays at three hundred million, so read the x3 row of your tables, and read the bad-harvest column. Rich, a thin year makes for very polite conversations, and I'll be wanting one with you about a Land Deed.",
 "Silke: A bad harvest hits everyone's purse, but I'm sitting on a hundred and two grain while Rich has twenty-five, so he'll want to sell that Deed this year more than ever. And with the Province 2 irrigation the only work still owed, this could be the last spring we get, so I have to get that Deed and the chair before anyone builds it.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out a bad harvest at x3. The new land bought last year pays 20 a square from this harvest; negative incomes are paid into the Harvest Pile.")
say("Rich", "The Pile pays out to the grain: Reina ten, Graham fifty, myself ten, Clark ninety, Noah seventy, Paddy thirty. Silke, ten in from you, and Rob, forty in from you, and I'll count it in front of everyone. Rent stays at five a head, my friends, the same five as always, because a bad year is no year to squeeze a man. Silke, my door's always open, and you'll find the Deeds are still fifty. Thin years don't make land any cheaper.",
 "Rich: Five from each is the most I can ask without a quarrel. With the Bowl in my cart that takes me to sixty and a half. Silke wants a Deed and I want her grain, so let her wait in the bad harvest and see whether fifty starts to look like a bargain. (From the board: the rents take Rich to 50.5.)")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich keeps the rent at 5, and Clark, Noah and Paddy each pay him 5.")
say("Reina", "A bad harvest is no year to squeeze anyone, darlings, so the tax is twenty, the lowest on the scale, and Graham, your irrigation is a gift from the crown, all thirty of it, and you won't pay a grain. Any land you want this year, the treasury pays for it in full. Nobody at this table will have a reason to sign anything.",
 "Reina: That's the last work owed, and the game ends the moment it's built, before the auction, so Silke never gets to show her set and Rich never gets to sell her that Deed. Twelve years planning, and the cart I'm watching this time is the one that closes the hall.")
say("Graham", "Twenty percent, fifty in all: Graham ten, Rich two, Clark eighteen, Noah fourteen, Paddy six, Silke nothing. I'll count it at the table and hand it over whole. Rob, I'll ask you anyway, for the record. Reina, about the irrigation: thank you, but no. Province 2 doesn't want it. A gift worth thirty that ends the game the moment it's dug, with you on the throne and me with an empty shelf, isn't a gift, it's the final entry in the minutes. Keep your thirty. I'll take the land you offered to pay for in full, though, all six squares. You said nobody would have a reason to sign anything, so I'm not signing for the ditch.",
 "Graham: The last public work ends the game, and the one sitting on the throne when it ends is Reina, so I'm refusing her generosity to keep the game going one more year. If she meant what she said about land, I get six free squares of income. If she didn't, everyone at the table just watched her generosity run out where it stopped helping her.")
taxes(.20, collector="Graham, the Official, collects the tax for the crown", hover="The Warlord's income is negative this year, so he owes nothing.")
act("Graham refuses the Province 2 Irrigation System. It is a negotiated work: Province 2 and the crown must both agree the split, and neither can force the other (Section 12). It is the last work owed, so while it stands unbuilt the game goes on.", map_hl=[2], big=True)
buy_land(2, ["E11","E12","E13","E14","E15","F10"], {1:120}, "New land: Graham buys six squares for Province 2, E11 to E15 and F10 on the strip below the Wall; the crown pays all 120, as Reina offered. Each adds 20 to Province 2's harvest from next year.")
say("Reina", "Graham darling, nobody gets an empty shelf for nothing. Thirty for the ditch and a hundred in your hand, counted out in front of everyone the moment the spade goes in. That's the best thirty-yard trench anyone in China has ever been paid to dig. And if you'd still rather not, the crown keeps its thirty and everyone's land is still on me this year, so nobody has a reason to sign anything.",
 "Reina: Grain only breaks ties, and the throne is what I'm here for, so a hundred to close the hall before Silke can show her set is cheap. If Graham says no, at least everyone at the table will have heard him turn down a hundred, and I'll be watching Rich's Deeds very closely at the auction.")
say("Graham", "A hundred grain is generous, Reina, and I've written it down. But you'd be paying me a hundred to close the book while you're on the last line of it, and grain doesn't buy anything once the game's over. The ditch stays undug. I'll take the three squares you've already said are on the crown, though. Thank you, and I mean that exactly as much as you meant the hundred.",
 "Graham: She's raised from thirty to a hundred and thirty, which tells me exactly what one more year is worth to her, so it's worth at least that much to me. The free land pays from next harvest, and next harvest is the only one I'm playing for.")
act("Graham refuses the second offer too. As Reina said, the crown builds nothing this year: the Province 2 Irrigation System, the last work owed, stays unbuilt, and the year goes on to the auction.", map_hl=[1,2])
buy_land(2, ["D15","D16","F9"], {1:60}, "New land: Graham buys three more squares for Province 2, D15, D16 and F9; the crown pays all 60.")
say("Rich", "Your Majesty is generosity itself, and I'd take every square you're offering if Province 3 had a single one left to give. But the desert's at my back and the Wall's over my head, and Graham's already been kind enough to sell me the last fields there were. So I'll sit and admire his new acres. Graham, a man who turns down a hundred grain on principle is a man I'll very much enjoy doing business with at the auction.",
 "Rich: I have nowhere left to buy, and with that ditch still undug every square is a gamble anyway, so I keep my forty-eight and a half for the auction. Graham has just told the whole table he won't let Reina close the book from the throne, and that makes him the man to watch, and also the man who'll want a Deed.")
say("Silke", "Your Majesty said in front of everyone that the treasury pays in full, and the Scholar writes down exactly what's said at this table. So that's I25, I26 and J26, three squares on my coast, sixty grain from the crown and nothing from me. Thank you, Reina. I'll read you a kinder card next spring.",
 "Silke: Free land is free land, even if Graham's ditch means it might never pay back. I'm not touching my ninety-two and a half grain, because I need fifty of it for Rich's Deed and twenty more for the set.")
buy_land(4, ["I25","I26","J26"], {1:60}, "New land: Silke buys three squares of sea for Province 4, I25, I26 and J26; the crown pays all 60.")
say("Clark", "Your Majesty said \"any land you want this year, the treasury pays in full,\" and Silke wrote it down, so I'm reading it back to her from page one. Eleven squares of sea off Province 5, two hundred and twenty from the crown, nothing from me. Reina, darling, that goes under \"admired\", and for once it doesn't go under \"owed\".",
 "Clark: Every square is free, so if Graham never digs his ditch I'm earning two hundred and twenty more each harvest, and if he does I've lost nothing at all. It also pulls two hundred and twenty out of Reina's purse right before an auction where she has to outbid people to keep her throne. (From the board: the Emperor doesn't bid at the auction.)")
buy_land(5, ["J27","K27","L27","M27","M28","N28","O28","P28","Q26","Q27","Q28"], {1:220}, "New land: Clark buys eleven squares of sea for Province 5, J27 to Q28; the crown pays all 220, and its reserve falls to 64.")
say("Noah", "Her Majesty said any land, paid in full, and Silke's got it in the book, so I'll keep it modest: six squares on my coast, R27, S27, T27 and U24 to U26. Six twenties is a hundred and twenty from the crown and nothing from me. And Graham, since somebody ought to say it, every grain the treasury spends today is a grain it hasn't got for paying you to dig that ditch.",
 "Noah: Free land is the one bargain at this table with no grain over the top. It's in the far corner where the boots can't reach, and every square the crown pays for leaves Reina a bit less to bribe her way to the end of the game with. (From the board: after Clark's eleven squares the crown holds 64, so it can't pay 120; Noah is told so.)")
say("Noah", "I stand corrected, the treasury's down to sixty-four after Clark's sea. So it's three squares, R27, S27 and T27, sixty from the crown and nothing from me, and that leaves Her Majesty four grain. Graham, I'm only saying the arithmetic, but four grain won't pay anyone a hundred to dig a ditch.",
 "Noah: Three free squares is as much free land as the crown can pay for, and I'm keeping my own grain for the Deed against Silke. Leaving the treasury on four also means Reina can't buy the end of the game this year.")
buy_land(6, ["R27","S27","T27"], {1:60}, "New land: Noah buys three squares for Province 6, R27, S27 and T27; the crown pays all 60, and its reserve falls to 4.")
say("Paddy", "I'd love more fields, Your Majesty, but you've four grain left, and I'll not be the man who takes your last one. Fifty-two and a half is what I've got, and I'm keeping it for a Customs House, a Deed or a Jar. Fields can wait. A set can't.",
 "Paddy: That ditch could get dug any year now, and a square I buy today might never pay me back a single harvest. So every grain stays in the purse for the market. Silke's one Deed short and Rich is sitting on three Deeds, so that's the pair I'm watching.")
say("Graham", "I start from zero again, so there are honest figures on every lot and I won't raise once I've reached them. Rich, I'll be at your table for the Brush and the Vase, and you know exactly how much I've got, because I said it out loud.",
 "Graham: The Brush and the Vase are wanted only by Rich and me, and he has forty-eight to spend, so those are the two I mean to win. I can't afford to stop Silke getting her Deed, and with three Deeds in Rich's stock it wouldn't hold her off for long anyway.")
say("Rich", "Graham, I admire a man who reads his figures out loud, so I'll do the same: a grain over and never two, and never more than I can spare. The Deeds are fifty again, as they were last spring. Silke, you'll notice the bad harvest didn't wash the price away. Paddy, Graham, if you want a Customs House or a Vase more than I do, I won't make you pay the Pile for the privilege.",
 "Rich: I'm four pieces and twenty grain short with forty-eight and a half in hand, so this year I'm shopping for bargains, not fighting wars, and every grain I keep counts if Graham ever lets that ditch be dug. The Deed stays at fifty. Whoever takes it pays me twelve and a half, and I'd rather they took the chair of someone with a full granary than mine.")
say("Silke", "One lot, one bid, and everyone at this table knows which one it is. Rich, your fifty is fair and I'll meet it. Noah, you're still a Jar short, so a Deed only gets you halfway to the set. My Brush goes up at fifteen. Graham, you said you'd be wanting one.",
 "Silke: Seventy-two is every grain I have except the twenty a full set needs, so this is everything I've got on a single lot. Noah's the only purse that can outbid me, and I'm betting he'd rather keep his grain for a Jar than pay the Pile to keep me out.")
say("Clark", "For the minutes: I'm bidding on three things, the Customs House, a Deed and the Bowl, because that's exactly what my shelf is missing, and I'll stop well before any of them gets silly. My Jar starts at thirty. Noah, Paddy, you both need one, and I'd hate to see either of you having to explain to the camera why you let the other man have it.",
 "Clark: Eleven squares of free sea put me at three hundred and thirty-five a year, so I don't have to win today, I only have to keep anyone else from winning cheaply. With Noah and Paddy both short a Jar, a thirty-grain floor ought to put a quarter of a decent price into my pocket. (From the board: the new squares pay from next year's harvest.)")
say("Noah", "Here's the arithmetic, since somebody has to say it out loud. Silke has ninety-two and a half and has to keep twenty back for her set, so seventy-two and a half is the most she can write for that Deed, and I've a hundred and forty-five and a half. Silke, you're right that a Deed only gets me halfway, but it gets you all the way, and I'd rather pay for half a set than watch you sit down in somebody's chair. My Bowl starts at ten, Clark, and that's a fair price for the last piece on your list.",
 "Noah: Seventy-three is one grain over the most Silke can pay and still keep her twenty. Whatever's left after the twenty I keep back goes on Clark's Jar, and if I lose that, at least the Deed in my corner is one Silke can't use.")
say("Paddy", "I'll be honest, since everyone else is: I can't make a set this year on fifty-two and a half, so I'm not going to pretend I can. I'll bid on a Customs House and a Jar, and the Deeds can go to whoever wants to pay Rich's fifty. My Vase goes up at ten, Graham, Rich, and that's a fair price from a man who paid a good deal more for his.",
 "Paddy: If I can get a Customs House and a Jar this year, I'm only a Deed short next spring with fifty-five coming in, and I'll show the set the minute I've got it this time. Silke's the one who's one piece short now, and I can't stop her with my purse, so I'm counting on Noah's arithmetic to do it for me.")
say("Rob", "Thirteen years, my friends, and the desert still hasn't raised its hand. You lot go ahead and buy the pots. Silke, those four in your front garden are lovely, really, and Rich, your Bowl on J17 is very well kept. I'll be calling round to admire them shortly, and I won't need a receipt.",
 "Rob: Six lots, six passes. Let them pour their grain into the Pile while W3 and W5 stand one step from Silke's row and three lads close in on Rich's Bowl. Two names left, and every one of them is in walking distance.")
act("Minimums: Graham's Customs House 5, Rich's Land Deed Scroll 50, Silke's Bamboo Brush 15, Clark's Dragon Jar 30, Noah's Wooden Bowl 10, Paddy's Vase 10. Rob passes on every lot.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7,8])
DG13 = "That's my figure. Take it with my blessing, and I'll remember the number."
DR13 = "Yours with my blessing. I never pay two over."
DS13 = "There's the thread again. Noah, I hope that Deed's heavy."
DC13 = "Noted, and carried forward. Enjoy it while it's cheap."
DP13 = "That's past what a wheat farmer can spend. It's yours, and good luck with it."
lot(2, "CH", [B(3,8), B(3,None,DR13), B(7,18), B(7,None,DP13), B(5,19)],
 hover="Graham's minimum was 5. Clark's limit was 28. Clark puts the Customs House on P25.", at="P25")
act("The Deed is close: Noah's limit is 73 and Silke's 72, so each is offered one raise.", map_hl=[4,6])
say("Silke", "Noah, you've done my sums for me, so here's one you didn't do. I don't have to sit down this year. I only have to hold that Deed when the harvest comes in. Eighty-five. If you want to pay the Pile more than that for half a set, the Deed is yours, and so is the explaining to the camera.",
 "Silke: At eighty-five I can't afford to show the set this year, but with seventy-five a year coming in I'd show it next spring, and Graham's refusal of the ditch means there probably is a next spring. If Noah goes over me, he's paid for half a set with grain he needs for his Jar, and either way I've made him pay for it.")
say("Noah", "Silke, ninety-two and a half is every grain you've got, so ninety-three is one more than you can write, and I'm saying it so nobody thinks I'm hiding anything. That leaves me fifty-two and a half, Clark, and that's what your Jar is going to have to settle for.",
 "Noah: Ninety-three is the only figure that makes this certain, because Silke can't beat it even by emptying her purse. A Deed in my corner is one less Deed in her garden, and I'll find the Jar money next spring if I have to.")
lot(3, "LD", [B(5,52), B(5,None,DC13), B(4,85), B(4,None,DS13), B(6,86)],
 hover="Rich's minimum was 50. Silke raised to 85 and Noah to 93; Clark's limit was 52. Noah puts the Deed on Q19.", at="Q19")
lot(4, "BB", [B(2,15)],
 hover="Silke's minimum was 15. Graham's limit was 22; Rich's 8 was under the minimum. Graham puts the Brush on G12.", at="G12")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(7,32), B(7,None,DP13), B(6,33)],
 hover="Clark's minimum was 30. Paddy's limit was 32; Noah's was 52.5; Graham's 16 and Rich's 6 were under the minimum. Noah puts the Jar on Q20.", at="Q20")
act("With the Deed and the Jar, Noah holds the Rice Peasant's full set: a Customs House, Bamboo Brush, Vase, Land Deed Scroll and Dragon Jar, with 26.5 grain. It is complete after the fourth lot, and no later lot this year completes anyone's set.", map_hl=[6], big=True)
say("Noah", "Silke, you were right, I paid the Pile more than any man should for half a set. But the Jar came cheap, so it's a whole set now: Customs House, Brush, Vase, Deed and Jar, with twenty-six and a half in hand. I'll take the Scholar's chair and its ninety-six, which makes me a hundred and twenty-two and a half, and I'm saying the figure out loud because I know exactly what this table does to a man sitting on a full purse.",
 "Noah: After thirteen years of losing by one grain, I paid a hundred and nineteen for the last two pieces, and it was worth it, because the Scholar's chair is the one that held the most grain and the one that was coming for my seat. The Brush goes back to its stock and I'm starting over, but this time I've done the sum for my own chair before I sat in it.")
swap(6, 4, "Noah Tall shows the Rice Peasant's complete set (Customs House, Bamboo Brush, Vase, Land Deed Scroll, Dragon Jar) and 26.5 grain, and replaces Silke Weber as the Scholar, the seat he drew. He keeps his grain and takes Silke's 96. The Brush, Province 4's own, goes back to its stock; his other four pieces move with him to Province 4. Silke returns her four pieces to stock, draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and plays the Rice Peasant, in Province 6, from now on; Province 6's Wooden Bowl is hers to sell.",
 hover="Section 11: a peasant may replace the Scholar. Noah is back in the seat he drew; Silke drew the Silk Peasant and now sits in Province 6. The later lots are settled from the same limits.",
 at={"CH":"J22", "VA":"K22", "LD":"M22", "DJ":"M23"})
lot(6, "WB", [B(5,18), B(5,None,DC13), B(2,19)],
 hover="Noah set the minimum at 10 before he moved; the Bowl is Province 6's, so Silke, now the Rice Peasant, is the seller. Clark's limit was 18; Graham's was 20. Graham puts the Bowl on G13.", at="G13")
lot(7, "VA", [B(2,10)],
 hover="Paddy's minimum was 10. Graham's limit was 22; Rich's 8 was under the minimum. Graham puts the Vase on G14.", at="G14")
say("Clark", "Graham's Customs House goes on P25, right next to my Brush, and it cost me nineteen, which is one more than Paddy and for once the \"by one\" went my way. Graham, you beat me to the Bowl by one, so you go on the old page, and I'm told the page is getting crowded.",
 "Clark: P25 keeps it out of every soldier's reach for two rounds, so three of my five pieces now sit on the back of the coast where Rob can't get at them. I need a Deed and a Bowl, and with three hundred and thirty-five coming in each harvest I can afford to wait for them.")
say("Graham", "Fifteen, nineteen and ten, which is forty-four for three pieces, and I haven't raised once. Noah, congratulations. I mean it, and I've noted where you're sitting. Rob, they're on the G column, so you'll have to admire them from a distance.",
 "Graham: That's three pieces on the back squares where no soldier can reach them for two rounds, and fifty-four grain left with a big harvest coming from the crown's free land. I still need a Deed and a Jar, which happen to be what everyone else wants too. (From the board: Graham holds 58.5.)")
say("Noah", "They stay where they stand, J22, K22, M22 and M23, every one out of reach of the boots this year, and I've counted the squares twice. Rob, I'll tell you what I told you in Year Four: I know exactly how many steps you are from my shelf. For the record, I'm a Bowl short and I hold a hundred and twenty-two and a half, and that's not a figure I'll be sitting on quietly.",
 "Noah: Funny thing, J22 and K22 are the same squares my Customs House and Deed sat on in Year Four, before Rob carried one off. Every square here is safe for one year and none for two, so next spring it's buy a Bowl fast or move house, and I'm watching Graham, who's a Deed and a Jar away and knows where I'm sitting.")
march({"W1":"I12", "W2":"J15", "W3":"I19", "W4":"L25", "W5":"L19"}, take={"W4":("VA","M18")}, text="Robert Land marches: W1 onto I12 in Province 2 (Graham pays 5); W2 onto J15; W3 onto I19 in Province 1 (the crown pays 10); W4 onto L25 in Province 5 and carries off Clark's Vase to M18 on the desert; W5 onto L19 in Province 4 (Noah pays 5).",
 hover="Rob: Three soldiers are converging on Rich's Bowl so one of them gets there whatever he does, and W1 is three steps from Graham's row. That's both my last names. Clark's Vase doesn't count for anything, but it costs him a set, and a villain has his standards to keep. (From the board: Clark was raided before, so the Vase adds nothing to Rob's objective. The crown held 4, so the raid on Province 1 puts it into debt.)")
say("Rob", "Graham, the G column's a lovely spot, and W1 has just started his walk to see it. He's billing you five at the gate on the way in. Rich, three of my lads are all heading for one Bowl, so put the kettle on. Clark, I'm sorry, darling, but your Vase was standing right next to my boy and he couldn't help himself. Write it on page one with the others.")
salaries({2:5, 4:5}, text="Salaries: Reina, the Emperor, declared 8 to the Official and 8 to the Scholar, but the crown is in debt after the raid on Province 1. The 5 each the Emperor owes is paid, deepening the debt; the 3 more each is a gift, which a player in debt can't give (Note 7). Graham, the Official, and Noah, now the Scholar, receive 5 each.",
 hover="The crown ends Year 13 at -16. A player in debt can't bid, buy, trade, give grain or fund works, and every bag received pays the debt first; debt is a cause for revolt (Section 16).")
